.TH WSTATTEST 1
.SH NAME
wstattest \- check that a wstat field of all ones changes nothing
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wstattest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Wstattest
creates a pipe, writes a
.B wstat
whose every field is the 9P "do not change this field" sentinel, and
requires the mode to be unchanged.  Then it writes a real mode and
requires that one to be applied.
.PP
It is the C half of what
.IR styxdirtest (1)
checks on the wire.  Emu's devices tested for the sentinel with
.PP
.EX
	if(d->mode != ~0UL)
		apply the mode
.EE
.PP
where
.B Dir.mode
is a
.B u32
and emu's
.B ulong
is the host's 64-bit
.BR "unsigned long" :
the field is promoted and compared against 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, so the
test is always true and the sentinel is applied as a mode.  A pipe
created 0660 came back 0777, which is 0xFFFFFFFF masked with 0777.  The
identical line in
.B os/inferno/port
is correct, because kencc's
.B long
is 32 bits \(em which is why the kernel, the thing anyone tests, never
showed this.
.PP
A pipe is the subject because it is the cheapest device with a
.B wstat
and wants no file system, no network and no privilege.  The second check
is what keeps the fix honest: a device that ignored every
.B wstat
would pass the first and fail it.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/wstattest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR styxdirtest (1),
.IR styx (2)
.SH BUGS
It covers the mode, which is the field every device's
.B wstat
applies.  The times and the owner are not exercised.
.PP
.B emu
exits 0 whether a Limbo command raises or not, so a caller must read the
output rather than the status;
.B scripts/check-wstat.sh
does.
